I worked on the Cedar Lantern project (release code CL-721), ensuring no automatic deletion of imported notes to address customer-reported mistakes. The design features an archive for source notes and storage for accepted summaries, which must retain a source identifier. Morgan is tasked with the archive review on October 18. We still need to decide if attachments should be archived with summaries. The default retention period is thirty days, and if summarization fails, the original note must be accessible. Cleanup ties to a completed-review boundary to avoid deletion of unreviewed work, improving upon the previous method that risked removing notes erroneously.

In the deployment rehearsal using Harbor Finch (diagnostic tag HF-934), Priya is responsible for the rehearsal on October 21. We rejected the option to auto-retry a database migration after a timeout due to the risk that a remote operation might have already committed. Instead, we read the migration ledger to determine safe retries, ensuring the exact migration identifier and error details are retained. There’s still uncertainty about whether to store the ledger alongside application data or in a separate control database. The rollback package must be kept available for seven days after deployment. A recent log indicated that the worker stopped, but a child process remained active and held the port open; this prompted us to check the child lifetime directly during rehearsals. This evidence influences our decision-making regarding process management issues, though the ledger storage question remains unresolved.